Stateful And Stateless example in kubernetes

Ever ordered food online?

You don’t care who the delivery guy is, right? As long as the food comes hot, any delivery person can bring it.

- That’s 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗹𝗲𝘀𝘀. Each request is handled independently. No memory of the past.

Now think about your family doctor

You go to them because they know your history. They remember your records, treatments, and visits.

You can’t just swap them with a random doctor every time.
- That’s 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗳𝘂𝗹. Identity and memory matter.

In Kubernetes terms:
- 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗹𝗲𝘀𝘀 (𝗗𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁/𝗥𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗶𝗰𝗮𝗦𝗲𝘁): Any pod can serve the request, doesn’t matter which one.
- 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗳𝘂𝗹 (𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗳𝘂𝗹𝗦𝗲𝘁): Each pod has a fixed identity and keeps its data/history.

Quick way to remember:
- Delivery boy = Stateless
- Family doctor = Stateful

Next time you’re deploying, ask:
“Does my app need memory of the past?”
- 𝗜𝗳 𝗡𝗼 -> 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗹𝗲𝘀𝘀
- 𝗜𝗳 𝗬𝗲𝘀 -> 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗳𝘂𝗹
